    // The stress it yields at goes from LAVA_YIELD to a million times it as it sets; the thickness rises a
    // hundredfold on the way. Those two curves are this page's.
    //
    // Ice (material 9) is that elastic solid too, from the first: stiff, and it does not flow at any stress. What it
    // does is break. A particle of it stretched past ICE_BREAK along any axis is broken for good: it forgets its
    // shape, and from then on it is rubble, a grain among grains (material 9.4: the sand's model, above), which
    // slides and piles but holds nothing together. So a block of ice is rigid until it is struck or bent, and then
    // comes apart along where it was pulled. A stretch as the test of breaking is the simplest there is (a maximum
